{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/jasonyandell/texas-42/issues/100","repository_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/jasonyandell/texas-42","labels_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/jasonyandell/texas-42/issues/100/labels{/name}","comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/jasonyandell/texas-42/issues/100/comments","events_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/jasonyandell/texas-42/issues/100/events","html_url":"https://github.com/jasonyandell/texas-42/issues/100","id":5672115556,"node_id":"I_kwDOTo4MPs8AAAABUhWdZA","number":100,"title":"Walt: a learned bottom rung (design note)","user":{"login":"jasonyandell","id":389660,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjM4OTY2MA==","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/389660?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/jasonyandell","html_url":"https://github.com/jasonyandell","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jasonyandell/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jasonyandell/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jasonyandell/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jasonyandell/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jasonyandell/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jasonyandell/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jasonyandell/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jasonyandell/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jasonyandell/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"labels":[],"state":"open","locked":false,"assignees":[],"milestone":null,"comments":0,"created_at":"2026-10-02T02:43:07Z","updated_at":"2026-10-02T02:43:07Z","closed_at":null,"assignee":null,"author_association":"OWNER","active_lock_reason":null,"sub_issues_summary":{"total":0,"completed":0,"percent_completed":0},"issue_dependencies_summary":{"blocked_by":0,"total_blocked_by":0,"blocking":0,"total_blocking":0},"body":"EXPLORATORY tier. Companion to #99 (the algorithm card). This is the one addition to walt that the measurements in `walt/probes/simple_walt/README.md` (branch `ccr-7425ff15-uya1yg`) point at, written for the general algorithm, not for 42. It applies to any game walt applies to: hidden deal, public actions.\n\n## Where it goes\n\nWalt is a ladder of best responses that bottoms out at random. The cost of the ladder is the bottom rung: one root decision spawns thousands of level-0 minds, each an exact search over its own sampled worlds against random play. Everything above the bottom is small. The bottom is also where the decision noise lives: the root's move flips on ~30% of decisions under an inner reseed, flat in every sample size tried (root deals 15–60, inner worlds 8–48, draws per world 1–16); reseeding the level-0 dice alone reproduces the whole floor; and about half of the minds' decisions are exact ties at 8 worlds.\n\nSo the proposal is: replace the level-0 mind's `evaluate` with a learned function, and change nothing else.\n\n## The split that makes it a drop-in\n\nToday a modeled seat's decision is one line: build the value vector, take the first max. Separate it:\n\n```\nevaluate(seat, private, public)  -> value per legal action     (expensive; the lawful part)\nselect(vector, key)              -> action                     (free; a stated rule)\n```\n\n`evaluate` reads only the seat's own information. `select` reads the vector and a key independent of the hidden deal (the ticker tape). Nothing in `decide`/`value` changes. Then:\n\n- the exact mind is `evaluate` = sample n0 worlds from my chair, solve each candidate jointly;\n- the learned mind is `evaluate` = net(private, public), one forward pass;\n- `select` is the same rule for both: the tape over the argmax set (a level-0 seat is dice over its legal set; a level-k seat is dice over the top of its evaluation — one mechanism, narrowed by thinking), or, if the net's output is used as weights, a distribution and the worlds carry rationals.\n\nThe root stays exact. Its modeled seats still partition its worlds by their move; the only change is how fast and how steadily each seat answers.\n\n## Why it can be better, not just faster\n\nThe exact 8-world mind is one noisy draw of the level-0 best response. Train the net on labels from 48- or 160-world minds (or from the strongest mind available: a δ-pruned exact search is a better teacher than a sampled one) and it learns the *expected* vector over the mind's belief: the infinite-sample level-0 mind, which cannot be run. Both inner noise sources disappear from the model, because the net is deterministic; what remains is the root's own sampling. Reproducibility comes for free.\n\nTwo properties it must keep:\n\n- **Lawful.** Input is the seat's own information only. Enforced by the input encoding, not by discipline.\n- **Support-safe.** The random bottom guarantees no lawful world is excluded from the root's partition (a deterministic bottom can exclude the true world). A learned bottom keeps this by returning weights, not a single action: p = softmax over the near-top, truncated and renormalized, mixed with a floor of uniform over legal. This is also where the \"compound result\" question from the discussion lands: the net outputs a distribution natively, so the weighted split is the natural selection rule and no tie rule is needed.\n\n## Shape, in general terms\n\n- **Input:** the information state as bits. Own holding (one bit per card/tile), what has been played, the current trick's cards in order, who leads, the running score relevant to the objective, the contract parameters, seat parity. For 42 that is about 150 bits; bridge is about 4× that. No hand-crafted features: the net's job is to be the heuristic we refused to write.\n- **Output:** one value per card/tile, masked to legal. Regress the pmake vector; do not classify the argmax. The vector is what `select` and the weighted split consume, and it is reusable by anything above.\n- **Size:** two hidden layers of 64, ~15k parameters for 42; a few hundred multiply-adds per call. Batchable: a root decision's thousands of minds are one `[minds, inputs]` matrix. Fits a phone or a WASM build.\n- **Arithmetic:** train in floats; quantize to int8 fixed-point for inference so the shipped player stays integer and the no-float discipline holds at the boundary that matters.\n\n## Data\n\nSelf-play with exact minds, logging every modeled decision as (information state, value vector). Labels from the strongest affordable mind. Hold out by deal, never by row (rows within a deal are correlated). The Rust walt produces millions of rows an hour; a bootstrap set of ~10^6 is plenty for 15k parameters.\n\n## The two tests, in order\n\n1. **Agreement:** top-action match rate with the exact labelling mind on held-out deals, and mean absolute error on the vector. Cheap; says whether the net learned the mind.\n2. **Strength:** walt-with-net-minds vs the community-approved walt on mirrored deals. The only number that matters. Note that reseed-vs-reseed is symmetric by construction and measures nothing; the comparison has to be against a different player.\n\n## What it unlocks\n\nOnce the bottom is O(1), a level-1 mind is cheap, so level-2 walt becomes affordable at the root: the ladder gets a rung back. And the net is the first socket for a thinking layer: anything that learns opponent tendencies or conditions on observed play is a change to the net's inputs or training set, never to the ladder.\n\n## Generality\n\nNothing here is 42-specific except the bit widths. The same `evaluate`/`select` split, the same lawfulness-by-encoding, the same support-safety rule and the same two tests apply to the spades and bridge branches. The one per-game choice is the objective the labels encode (pmake, tricks, points), which is the root's objective, inherited unchanged.","closed_by":null,"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/jasonyandell/texas-42/issues/100/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"timeline_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/jasonyandell/texas-42/issues/100/timeline","performed_via_github_app":null,"state_reason":null,"pinned_comment":null}